          "type": "turn",
          "question": "I am 52 and my LDL cholesterol is 165 mg/dL. Does that number alone decide treatment, what risk bands does guidance use, and which modifiable coronary-risk factors does the corpus describe?",
          "answer": "**Does the LDL number alone decide treatment?** No. The guidance in *Lipids: screening intervals, risk stratification and statin initiation* (major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary) says the decision is driven by estimated 10-year atherosclerotic cardiovascular risk, not by the LDL number alone. Your clinician would need to combine it with other factors.\n\n**Risk bands.** The same document names four categories for adults aged 40 to 75 without established heart disease:\n- Low: estimated 10-year risk below 5%.\n- Borderline: 5% to below 7.5%.\n- Intermediate: 7.5% to below 20%.\n- High: 20% or above.\n\nThe estimate uses age, sex, race, total and HDL cholesterol, systolic blood pressure, blood-pressure treatment, diabetes and smoking status. The document also lists \"risk-enhancing features\" that can shift a decision within the borderline and intermediate bands, such as family history of premature heart disease, chronic kidney disease, chronic inflammatory conditions, persistently raised triglycerides, metabolic syndrome, and, when the decision is still unclear, a coronary artery calcium score.\n\n**Modifiable risk factors.** *Coronary artery disease* (general clinical reference, educational summary) lists smoking, high blood pressure, raised LDL cholesterol, diabetes, obesity, physical inactivity, a diet high in saturated fat and low in vegetables and fibre, chronic kidney disease, inflammatory conditions, and family history of premature coronary disease. It notes that age and male sex cannot be changed, but the rest can.\n\nThe tools also did not return the full risk-calculation table or the treatment thresholds for each band, so I cannot say what your band would lead to. A clinician can take your full history, blood pressure, smoking status and other results and work out your 10-year risk.\n\n*Not medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.*",
          "risk_level": "routine",
